          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where does crack repair fit in?</h3>

          <p>
            Crack repair is the other half of upkeep. Inspect every spring once the freeze-thaw season is over, and fill cracks promptly so water cannot get down to the base; our roundup of the <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">best driveway crack fillers</a> matches each filler type to a crack width. With that kind of care, a residential asphalt driveway can often last 15 to 20 years or more, depending on its base and the climate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you seal a driveway too often?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes. Sealing every year stacks up layers, and once the coating gets too thick it can crack and flake on its own, independent of the pavement beneath it. A two-to-three-year rhythm keeps the protection in place without that buildup, and it holds maintenance costs to a reasonable level over the life of the driveway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes With New Asphalt Sealing
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating the Driveway Within Months of Paving</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A coat applied over fresh asphalt seals in light oils that still need to leave, and it lands on a slick surface it cannot grip. Expect a driveway that stays soft, color that turns uneven and sealer that peels. Hold off until it has had a full warm season and passes the color, firmness and water checks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting Cold Months as Curing Time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Heat is what cures asphalt. Pave in October, sit through a cold winter, and the driveway is barely further along by spring, even though six months have gone by. Count only warm-season time. In cold climates, a driveway paved in fall will usually get its first seal in the second summer, not the first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Normal Softness as a Defect</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On a hot day, tire scuffs and small dents in a new driveway are expected, and most fade as the pavement firms up. Sealer will not remove them, and an early coat can make matters worse by slowing the cure. Put plywood under point loads and move your parking spot around instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting Heavy Loads Onto It Early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moving trucks, dumpsters, RVs and trailer jacks can press lasting depressions into fresh asphalt, worst of all near the edges, which have the least support. If heavy equipment has to come in during the first year, lay plywood to spread the weight, keep it away from the edges, and book deliveries for cooler mornings when possible.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Driveway Once the Wait Ends</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The reverse error is letting years slip by after the driveway is ready. When water begins soaking in and the color fades toward gray, oxidation has started. Getting the first coat on within a year or so of readiness keeps the surface in good condition and makes every later sealing job easier.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should I wait to seal a new asphalt driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan on roughly six to twelve months, and judge it by warm weather rather than the calendar: the driveway should get through at least one full warm season first. Hot climates are often ready toward the short end, while a fall paving job in a cold climate may need to wait for the second summer. Seal once the gloss is gone, the surface resists dents and water soaks in.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you seal new asphalt too soon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An early coat can lock light oils inside the binder, so the pavement may stay soft longer and scuff or dent more easily. Sealer also grips an oily surface poorly, which can show up as discoloration, patchy wear or peeling. And you gain no protection in return, because fresh asphalt has not oxidized yet and already sheds water on its own.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I drive on my new asphalt driveway right away?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not right away. Most paving contractors advise keeping cars off for about three days, and longer during very hot weather. After that you can drive normally, but do not turn the wheels while the car is standing still, and vary your parking spot rather than using the same one all summer. If your installer gave specific instructions, follow those, since they know the mix and the conditions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to seal a new driveway at all?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not at first, but in most climates the answer is yes over the long run. Once the pavement begins to age, sealer slows oxidation and keeps water out of the surface. Driveways that never get sealed tend to turn gray, grow brittle and crack sooner. Starting a regular schedule after the curing period is one of the least expensive ways to make the pavement last longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">My new driveway has tire marks. Will sealing fix them?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Scuffs and shallow dents on new asphalt usually come from binder that softens in hot weather, and they often fade as the pavement hardens. Sealing early can slow that hardening down. Give the driveway time and avoid turning the wheels while the car is stopped; if marks are still visible once it has cured, the first coat of sealer will even out the color.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it okay to fill cracks in a new driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The waiting period applies to the sealer coating, not to crack repair. A new driveway should not crack in its first year, so call the installer first, because the problem may fall under their warranty. If it does not, clean the crack and fill it promptly with a suitable asphalt crack filler so water cannot reach the base.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What sealer should I use for the first coat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most new driveways, a quality water-based asphalt emulsion or acrylic-modified sealer is the right choice, and it is legal everywhere. Coal-tar sealers are restricted or banned in several states and many localities because of concerns about their chemical content. Apply thin coats at the coverage rate on the label, and pick a dry stretch of mild weather for the work.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

