        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Skip the sealer during a new driveway's first months; an early coat causes more trouble than it prevents. Give the pavement one full warm season to cure, keep point loads and scuffs off the soft surface, and seal only when the gloss has faded, the surface no longer dents and water has started to soak in.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Once those checks agree, apply a quality water-based sealer in thin coats on a dry, mild day. From then on, resealing every two to three years and filling cracks each spring is what carries a residential driveway to 15 or 20 years and beyond.
          </p>
